With $B_2$ determined, the oscillatory correction $V_2$ and its pressure $\pi_2$ solve $$\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 D_tV_2+(\nabla U)V_2+\kappa\partial_s\pi_2
   &=-(H_2-\langle H_2\rangle_s)-(B_2\cdot\kappa)\partial_s(\widetilde\chi A_1),\\
 \kappa\cdot V_2&=0,\qquad \langle V_2\rangle_s=0.
 \end{split} \tag{1.12}
\end{equation}$$ The mean $B_2$ transports the leading wave’s fast phase. This term has zero phase average and, like the oscillatory part $H_2-\langle H_2\rangle_s$, belongs to level two, so we solve for the mean $B_2$ before the oscillatory correction $V_2$. At fixed phase along each trajectory, (1.12) is a forced Craik–Criminale amplitude equation. Every wave uses zero initial data for this correction. The curl construction of Subsection 3.1 gives the divergence correction $C_3$ of $V_2$, so that $V_2+C_3$ is exactly divergence free. The term $C_3$ belongs to level three, where we solve for the mean and oscillatory corrections again.

